WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 258: Mark Twain: The Riverboat Pilot, Huckleberry Finn, and the Wit of America

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.280
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the father of American literature.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:21.360
[Rebecca] The steamboat pilot, humorist and satirist, whose razor-sharp wit and boundless compassion forged an authentic national voice.

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Mark Twain.

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:42.400
[George] Born in November 1835 in the tiny village of Florida, Missouri, on the exact night Halle's comet blazed across the heavens, Samuel Langhorn Clemens grew up in the bustling river Port of Hannibal on the western bank of the Mississippi River.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:54.640
[George] The dusty riverfront, limestone caves, and forested islands formed the idyllic backdrop of his youth, but the sunny surface concealed the brutal realities of frontier life and human slavery.

8
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:11.440
[George] When his father died in 1847, 11-year-old Sam left school to apprentice as a printer's devil, setting lead type for his older brother Orion's newspaper, the Hannibal Western Union, working with printers' type cases across New York, Philadelphia, and St.

9
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:20.320
[George] Louis, Sam developed an extraordinary ear for regional dialects, local idioms, and the rhythmic cadences of American speech.

10
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:33.040
[George] In 1857, while traveling downriver toward New Orleans with dreams of hunting coca leaves in the Amazon, Clemens was captivated anew by the majestic paddle wheel steamboats ploughing the muddy current.

11
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:47.040
[George] He apprenticed himself to Horace Bixby, a master pilot, spending two grueling years memorizing every snag, shifting sandbar, bluff, and sunken wreck along 1200 miles of the ever-changing Mississippi River.

12
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:57.120
[George] Navigating through pitch-black foggy nights and roaring spring floods, Clemens became a licensed pilot, listening to the leadman call out water depths into the darkness.

13
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.800
[George] Mark 1, Mark Twain, the cry signifying two fathoms or twelve feet, meaning safe water.

14
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:22.200
[George] Young Sam's adventures along the limestone bluffs and secluded islands of Hannibal instilled in him a permanent fascination with the boundless frontier, learning from escaped slaves and river boatmen the oral traditions, ghost tales, and spiritual melodies that later animated his finest prose.

15
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:32.760
[George] His time as a printer's devil exposed him to the daily machinery of political broadsides, developing his acute skepticism of partisan cant and sanctimonious moralizing.

16
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:46.360
[George] When the Civil War erupted in 1861, commercial steamboat traffic on the Mississippi ceased, following a comical, chaotic two-week stint in a Confederate militia unit that did little more than retreat through Missouri mud.

17
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:55.320
[George] Clemens boarded a stagecoach with his brother Orion and fled westward across the plains to the silver boomtown of Virginia City in the Nevada Territory.

18
00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:07.240
[George] Caught up in mining fever on the Comstock load, Clemens lost everything speculating in mining claims before taking a job as a city editor for the Territorial Enterprise newspaper in 1862.

19
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:12.040
[George] It was here that he first adopted his immortal pen name, Mark Twain.

20
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:21.080
[George] In 1865, while living in California gold mining camps, Twain heard a whimsical tavern yarn about a gambler and a pet frog.

21
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:32.120
[George] He penned the celebrated jumping frog of Calavera's County, a masterwork of deadpan Western humor published in New York that turned Twain into an overnight national sensation.

22
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:48.480
[George] In 1867, Twain embarked on the steamship Quaker City for a five-month Mediterranean cruise to the Holy Land, sending back hilarious, irreverent travel letters that poked merciless fun at European cultural pretension and American tourist gullibility.

23
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:55.120
[George] Published in 1869 as The Innocents Abroad, the best-selling book of his entire lifetime.

24
00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:08.960
[George] In the silver mining camps of Virginia City, Clemens embraced the rough and dready fraternity of miners, saloonkeepers and gamblers, polishing his unique comedic style of understated hyperbole and deadpan earnestness.

25
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:21.120
[George] The instantaneous success of the jumping frog proved to Eastern literary critics that a vital, unpretentious American literature was emerging from the wild mining camps and river bottoms of the American West.

26
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:34.400
[George] While aboard the Quaker City, Twain saw an ivory miniature portrait of young Olivia Langdon, the daughter of a wealthy, abolitionist coal merchant in Elmira, New York, and instantly fell madly in love.

27
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:45.680
[George] After courting her with over 100 passionate letters, they were married in February 1870, establishing a happy, devoted domestic partnership that lasted 34 years.

28
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:55.040
[George] Olivia became Twain's beloved muse, gentle editor, and moral anchor, lovingly pruning profanity and coarseness from his draft manuscripts.

29
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:09.920
[George] Settling in Hartford, Connecticut, Twain constructed an extravagant, 19room Victorian Gothic mansion featuring whimsical turrets, brick chimneys, and an expansive third floor billiard room where he wrote his greatest masterpieces.

30
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.960
[George] Surrounded by his three daughters, Sussie, Clara and Jean, Twain entered his golden literary decade.

31
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:28.720
[George] In 1873, collaborating with his neighbor Charles Dudley Warner, Twain published The Gilded Age, a tale of today, a searing satire of corrupt Washington politicians.

32
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:38.440
[George] Greedy railroad lobbyists, and speculative fever that permanently bestowed its name upon the late 19th century era of rapacious American capitalism.

33
00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:50.680
[George] Olivia's gentle guidance provided Sam with the emotional equilibrium and artistic discipline he desperately needed, transforming a nomadic Western journalist into the most respected man of letters in the nation.

34
00:05:50.680 --> 00:06:05.960
[George] The Hartford Mansion became a vibrant intellectual salon, welcoming international guests including Frederick Douglass, William Dean Howells, and Harriet Beecher Stowe, who engaged in lively midnight discussions over tobacco and billiard games.

35
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:14.520
[George] In 1876, Twain published The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, a nostalgic celebration of childhood along the Mississippi.

36
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:28.200
[George] But in its groundbreaking 1884 sequel, Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, Twain achieved an artistic triumph that Ernest Hemingway later declared was the fountainhead from which all modern American literature flows.

37
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:45.480
[George] Narrated entirely in the authentic, ungrammatical dialect of an illiterate, outcast white boy fleeing an abusive father, the novel charts Huck's perilous journey down the Mississippi River on a wooden raft alongside Jim, an enslaved black man escaping toward freedom in Ohio.

38
00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:52.520
[George] As they drift downriver beneath the quiet stars, Huck undergoes an agonizing internal moral crisis.

39
00:06:52.520 --> 00:07:06.120
[George] Torn between the legal teachings of church and society, which decreed that helping an escaped slave was a mortal sin deserving eternal damnation in hell, and his growing fraternal love and respect for Jim's profound humanity.

40
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:16.320
[George] In the novel's climactic moral climax, Huck tears up the letter betraying Jim to his owners, famously declaring, All right then, I'll go to hell.

41
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:29.440
[George] In that single revolutionary sentence, Twain struck a devastating artistic blow against institutional racism, establishing conscience over conventional law as the supreme moral law of the human heart.

42
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:38.000
[George] The moral genius of Huckleberry Finn lay in Twain's refusal to preach, by seeing the world through the uncorrupted eyes of a homeless boy.

43
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:46.720
[George] The novel exposed the monstrous hypocrisy of a slaveholding Christian society that could quote scripture while tearing families apart on the auction block.

44
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:58.080
[George] Hemingway's praise recognized that Twain had liberated American prose from European aristocratic elegance, replacing it with the direct, muscular poetry of the American vernacular.

45
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:06.160
[George] Despite his colossal literary royalties, Twain possessed an impulsive, reckless streak when it came to financial speculation.

46
00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:22.640
[George] Between 1880 and 1894, he poured his entire personal fortune, over $300,000, equivalent to $10 million today, into the Page Compositor, an overly complex, doomed mechanical typesetting machine invented by James W.

47
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.120
[George] Page that broke down constantly.

48
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.960
[George] Simultaneously, his own publishing company, Charles L.

49
00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.480
[George] Webster and Company, collapsed into bankruptcy following the Panic of 1893.

50
00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:46.560
[George] At age 59, broke, deeply in debt, and facing public humiliation, Twain resolved to repay every single creditor in full, refusing to hide behind bankruptcy protection.

51
00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:53.040
[George] In July 1895, accompanied by Olivia and daughter Clara, Twain launched a grueling.

52
00:08:53.040 --> 00:09:06.440
[George] Year-long around the world lecture tour across North America, the Pacific, Australia, New Zealand, India, and South Africa, packing lecture halls with his brilliant comedic monologues and razor sharp parables.

53
00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:21.960
[George] The tour was a triumphant commercial resurrection that repaid every dollar of his debts, but tragedy struck in London when word arrived that his favorite 24-year-old daughter Susie had died suddenly of spinal meningitis in Hartford, shattering Twain's heart.

54
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:36.280
[George] Twain's Around the World Lecture Tour became an epic personal odyssey of redemption, packing crowded theaters from Melbourne and Calcutta to Durban and London with spellbound audiences captivated by his dry humor and melancholic wisdom.

55
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:46.600
[George] The tragic loss of Sussey cast an enduring shadow over his remaining years, deepening the philosophical poignancy that imbued his later reflections with heartbreaking gravity.

56
00:09:46.600 --> 00:10:00.280
[George] Returning to America in 1900 as an international living legend, Twain adopted his iconic sartorial trademark, a tailored, dazzling white linen suit, worn year-round to celebrate his nonconformity.

57
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:13.560
[George] As the United States embarked on overseas imperial expansion following the Spanish-American War, annexing the Philippines, Puerto Rico, and Hawaii, Twain emerged as the fierce moral conscience of the nation.

58
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:28.520
[George] Serving as vice president of the American Anti Imperialist League from 1901 until his death, Twain wielded his pen like a broad sword, publishing blistering polemics like, To the Person Sitting in Darkness, and King Leopold's soliloquy.

59
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:34.600
[George] Exposing the bloody atrocities committed by Western colonial powers in the Philippines and the Congo.

60
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:48.880
[George] Twain fiercely insisted that the American Republic could not build an overseas empire without destroying its own founding ideals of liberty and self-government, warning that draping conquest in the language of civilization was pure hypocrisy.

61
00:10:48.880 --> 00:11:01.680
[George] In his anti-imperialist writings, Twain warned that the greatest danger to the American Republic came not from foreign invasion, but from the moral corruption of its own democratic soul through overseas conquest.

62
00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:09.840
[George] His searing essays in the North American Review exposed how corporate greed and military hubris masqueraded as national honor.

63
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:19.120
[George] Urging Americans to remember that true patriotism means loyalty to the country's founding principles rather than unquestioning obedience to the government of the day.

64
00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:31.120
[George] In 1909, observing that Halla's Comet was scheduled to return the following year, 74-year-old Mark Twain mused with poetic premonition to his biographer Albert Bigelow Payne.

65
00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.320
[George] I came in with Halla's Comet in 1835.

66
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.320
[George] It is coming again next year, and I expect to go out with it.

67
00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:42.800
[George] It will be the greatest disappointment of my life if I don't go out with Halle's Comet.

68
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:49.200
[George] The Almighty has said, no doubt, here are these two unaccountable freaks, they came in together.

69
00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.040
[George] They must go out together.

70
00:11:51.040 --> 00:12:04.640
[George] On April 21, 1910, one day after the comet reached perihelion at its closest approach to the sun, Samuel Langhorn Clemens closed his eyes and died peacefully at his Stormfield estate in Reading, Connecticut.

71
00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:14.480
[George] Thousands filed past his open bronze casket in New York's brick Presbyterian Church, while heads of state and ordinary working citizens wept across the globe.

72
00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:25.600
[George] Mark Twain did not merely entertain the world, he liberated the American voice, demonstrating that humor could be the ultimate weapon of truth, justice, and human decency.

73
00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:33.880
[George] As long as rivers flow and free spirits dream under the stars, Mark Twain's laughter and conscience will echo across the American soul.

74
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:49.880
[George] When Mark Twain died, world leaders from King Edward VII to President William Howard Taft delivered solemn tributes, while millions of ordinary factory workers, sailors, and schoolchildren wept for the whitehaired humorist who had made them laugh and think.

75
00:12:49.880 --> 00:13:01.720
[George] His immortal spirit remains woven into the fabric of the American imagination, reminding every generation that truth, courage, and laughter are the greatest gifts of free human beings.

76
00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:05.480
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

77
00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:12.520
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non president American series continues with episode 259.

78
00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:18.280
[Rebecca] Edgar Allan Poe, The Master of the Macabra, The Raven, and The Mystery at Baltimore.

79
00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.760
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

